nd this is because  Staphylococcus aureus produces two types  of coagulates so you carry off the  coagulase test there's two different  forms we'll talk about in a second and  that will help you to determine whether  your specimen is Staphylococcus aureus  or something kind of more benign like  Staphylococcus epidermidis now staph  produces two types of coagulase there is  bound coagulase that stuff is tightly  bound to their surface this is sometimes  referred to as clumping factor and for  clumping factor you use the slide test  and they can also produce some free  coagulase that just kind of gets um  secreted into the medium and for that we  use the two tests so now let's talk  about these two different kinds of tests  typically what's conducted first is the  slide test this is where you have a  slide you have some drops of saline on  the slide and so you add the test  organism to the Saline and then you add  a few drops of plasma typically rabbit  plasma is used all of other kinds can  and then you rock the slide back and  forth not for very long  for about ten seconds and so you're just  rocking that slide sort of gently up and  down if it's a coagulase negative  organism then there would be no clumps  however if it's coagulates positive if  organism produces this enzyme coagulates  then you should see clumps forming in  the saline plasma mixture within that  ten second period so it's very rapid and  pretty inexpensive enough that's a great  thing for a diagnostic test now if you  get a negative result you're supposed to  then carry out the tube test just to be  certain  just to give sort of an added added  evidence for coagulase negative activity  and so it was a tube test you have a  tube again with rabbit plasma and you  inoculate it with the test organism and  then you've incubated at 37 degrees  Celsius which is human body temperature  for about 90 minutes so you can see this  one the slide test you got your results  in ten seconds  this one is more on sor